---
// /known-issues — the public technical record.
//
// WHY THIS EXISTS (2026-08-24). A defect that materially affects reproducibility or a correctness
// claim has to be findable by the people relying on it, and the honest place for that is one page
// that says so plainly — not a banner on every affected surface, and not a private ledger. The
// repo's own ledgers (specs/open_forks.md, specs/doctrine_gaps.md) are where forks are ADJUDICATED;
// this page is where the ones that reach a reader are DISCLOSED.
//
// WHAT IT IS NOT: a changelog, a roadmap, a support queue, or a mirror of the GitHub issue tracker.
// An item earns a row here by materially affecting reproducibility, a correctness claim, or an
// exhibit. Everything else belongs in the release notes or the tracker.
//
// REGISTER (house rule v0.3 §4.3, and Huayin's Unit-C classification). This page is mostly TYPED
// FACTUAL STATUS: exact dependency and version, exact status, exact affected surface, exact
// reproduction result, exact upstream link. The `fields` list of each item carries those. The
// `explanation` is PLAIN PROSE and does the teaching. No theatrical language, no blame language, no
// vulnerability-style alarm treatment — a reader should be able to tell in ten seconds whether an
// item affects them, and read two paragraphs if it does.
//
// STATUS VOCABULARY — four states, deliberately. OPEN: established, not yet resolved, no complete
// mitigation. UPSTREAM: the resolution lies in a dependency and is tracked there. CONTAINED: a
// mitigation is in place and the residual behaviour is bounded and disclosed. RESOLVED: closed, kept
// for the record. An item states one; more states get minted only when an item cannot honestly wear
// one of these.
//
// NO PUBLICATION FACTS ARE AUTHORED HERE. This page cites software versions and issue numbers, not
// deposited editions, so it states no publication fact and takes no consumer permission — the
// ownership boundary settled in Repair Unit 2.
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const STATUSES = [
  ['OPEN',      'Established and not yet resolved. No complete mitigation is in place.'],
  ['UPSTREAM',  'The resolution lies in a dependency and is tracked there.'],
  ['CONTAINED', 'A mitigation is in place; the residual behaviour is bounded and disclosed.'],
  ['RESOLVED',  'Closed. Kept here as a record.'],
];

const ISSUES = [
  {
    id: 'hll-distinct-reproducibility',
    title: 'HLL approximate distinct counts are not currently reproducible across identical runs',
    status: 'OPEN',
    fields: [
      ['Discovered',        '24 August 2026'],
      ['Dependency',        'datasketches 5.2.0 (Python binding)'],
      ['Affected path',     'columna-core sketch-witness merges — the distinct-count family (hll_count → hll_merge → hll_estimate)'],
      ['Affected surface',  'Any approximate distinct count served through that path, including the buyers-by-month exhibit'],
      ['Reproduction',      'Reproduced outside Columna, in a program using only DataSketches'],
      // Resolution is deliberately EMPTY rather than absent: a field that will carry a fact later
      // says so now, so a reader can tell "not yet" from "not applicable".
      ['Upstream',          { href: 'https://github.com/apache/datasketches-python/issues/68',
                              text: 'apache/datasketches-python#68 — API-parity request' }],
      ['Resolution',        null, 'open'],
    ],
    explanation: [
      `A distinct count on this path is not counted exactly. It is estimated from a HyperLogLog
       sketch, and the estimate travels with its relative error, which is stated on the answer
       itself. That much is by design and is unchanged.`,
      `What we did not know until now is that the estimate is also sensitive to the order in which
       the underlying sketches are merged. Apache DataSketches offers two estimators for the same
       sketch. The HIP estimator is more accurate but order-dependent. The composite estimator is
       order-independent, at a modest cost in accuracy. Which one a caller gets is decided by the
       library: the default returns the best available, which is HIP where HIP is valid. The
       maintainers describe this as a deliberate trade-off between accuracy and order-independence,
       and the choice between the two estimators is exposed in the C++ and Java APIs.`,
      `The Python binding we use does not currently expose that choice. It offers the default
       estimator only, so a Python caller cannot select the order-independent one. Because the merge
       order of the underlying sketches is not fixed, the practical consequence is that two runs over
       identical logical input can return different distinct-count estimates.`,
      `Both estimates remain approximate results within their disclosed error bounds, and we have no
       finding that either is wrong. What is affected is reproducibility: an approximate distinct
       count served through this path should not currently be assumed to be byte-for-byte
       reproducible for identical logical input.`,
      `Columna has not changed its semantics to accommodate this. We have not imposed a canonical
       merge order to hold the order-dependent estimator steady, and we have not used the
       self-union trick that forces the library onto its order-independent estimator. Either would
       make our recording look more deterministic than the computation underneath it. We have asked
       upstream for the estimator controls that already exist in the other bindings, and until a
       supported way to make that choice explicitly is available in Python, the limitation is
       disclosed here rather than concealed in the code.`,
    ],
  },
];
---
<BaseLayout title="Known issues — datumwise"
  description="The public record of confirmed defects and active technical investigations that materially affect reproducibility, correctness claims, or exhibits in Columna and on this site."
  footer="columna">
  <div class="page-head">
    <p class="kicker">Known issues</p>
    <h1 class="serif">Known issues</h1>
    <p class="orientation">
      This page is the public record of confirmed defects and active investigations that materially
      affect reproducibility, a correctness claim, or an exhibit. It is not a changelog, a roadmap,
      or a mirror of our issue tracker — an item appears here when it changes what you can rely on.
    </p>
  </div>

  <section class="key">
    <dl>
      {STATUSES.map(([name, meaning]) => (
        <>
          <dt><span class={`badge badge-${name.toLowerCase()}`}>{name}</span></dt>
          <dd>{meaning}</dd>
        </>
      ))}
    </dl>
  </section>

  {ISSUES.map((issue) => (
    <article class="issue" id={issue.id}>
      <h2><a href={`#${issue.id}`}>{issue.title}</a></h2>
      <p class="status-line">
        <span class={`badge badge-${issue.status.toLowerCase()}`}>{issue.status}</span>
      </p>
      <dl class="facts mono">
        {issue.fields.map(([k, v, pending]) => (
          <>
            <dt>{k}</dt>
            <dd>
              {v == null
                ? <span class="pending">— {pending}</span>
                : typeof v === 'string'
                  ? v
                  : <a href={v.href} rel="noopener" target="_blank">{v.text}</a>}
            </dd>
          </>
        ))}
      </dl>
      {issue.explanation.map((para) => <p class="explain">{para}</p>)}
    </article>
  ))}
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .page-head { width: min(100% - 3rem, var(--measure)); margin: 0 auto; }
  .page-head h1 { font-size: clamp(1.8rem, 4vw, 2.4rem); margin: 0.3rem 0 0.5rem; }
  .kicker { color: var(--ink-faint); }
  .orientation { font-size: 0.95rem; color: var(--ink-soft); }

  .key { width: min(100% - 3rem, var(--measure)); margin: 2rem auto 0;
    padding-top: 1.2rem; border-top: 1px solid var(--rule); }
  .key dl { display: grid; grid-template-columns: max-content 1fr; gap: 0.4rem 1rem; margin: 0; }
  .key dd { margin: 0; font-size: 0.88rem; color: var(--ink-soft); }

  .issue { width: min(100% - 3rem, var(--measure)); margin: 2.5rem auto 0;
    padding-top: 1.5rem; border-top: 1px solid var(--rule); }
  .issue h2 { font-size: 1.15rem; line-height: 1.35; margin: 0 0 0.6rem; }
  .issue h2 a { color: inherit; text-decoration: none; }
  .issue h2 a:hover { color: var(--ink-soft); }
  .status-line { margin: 0 0 1rem; }

  /* Status is a LABEL, not an alarm: one weight, one muted ink, a hairline box. Nothing red. */
  .badge { display: inline-block; font-family: var(--mono, monospace); font-size: 0.72rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em; padding: 0.12rem 0.45rem; border: 1px solid var(--rule);
    border-radius: 2px; color: var(--ink-soft); }

  .facts { display: grid; grid-template-columns: max-content 1fr; gap: 0.35rem 1.2rem;
    margin: 0 0 1.4rem; font-size: 0.8rem; }
  .facts dt { color: var(--ink-faint); }
  .facts dd { margin: 0; color: var(--ink-soft); }
  .pending { color: var(--ink-faint); }

  .explain { font-size: 0.95rem; color: var(--ink-soft); margin: 0 0 0.9rem; }
</style>
